   "text": {
    "L1": "A freely suspended bar magnet stops pointing roughly north-south.",
    "L2": "Its end pointing north is called the north, or north-seeking, pole.",
    "L3": "A compass has a small magnetised needle that turns freely and then settles.",
    "L4": "Turn the compass so N on the dial lines up with the needle's north end, then read the direction.",
    "L5": "Keep the compass away from magnets and iron or steel, because they disturb the needle.",
    "L6": "The needle points north because Earth behaves like a giant magnet, not because the north is cold.",
    "L7": "You can make a simple compass by magnetising a steel pin and floating it on cork in water.",
    "L8": "Now use the steps to find which direction a classroom door faces."
   },
